From: pgf@srl02.cacs.usl.edu (Phil G. Fraering)
Date: Sat, 13 Aug 1994 20:12:41 -0500
Subject: [#94-8-154] ECON: inflation question

(Note: I saw Ray talking about this on usenet, but my follow-up failed
and I suddenly realized I didn't really want to post about it anyway;
I did want to talk about this subject before seeing his post, though.
My apologies to whoever is seeing this twice).

I need a label or word for a phenomenon that I can best describe now
as non-fiat-currency inflation. While outlining an essay I'm currently
writing on Apollo, comets, and whether there will be a future space
program worth the name, I started thinking about the old Apollo
program vs. the pyramids analogy, and I remembered something else
about the pyramids and other 'cyclopean architecture.'

There are lots and lots of assorted crackpots who have no real
conception of how the pyramids could be built, and postulate "lost
physics" they believe necessary in order to build them and other
megalithic structures constructed by neolithic and/or bronze-age
societies around the world. 

(NOTE: they don't call it "lost physics" but rather "lost technology."
While I think there may _possibly_ be lost technology, I doubt it used
any sort of physics that isn't ladled out daily in high schools around
the nation.)

I think that the perceptual blinders making these people search for
UFO's or mystical sound waves may be a simple barrier of a thousand
years of inflation in the price of labor. I think the price of labor
goes up when the number of commodities or consumer goods, or
categories of commodities or consumer goods, increaces. It seems to me
that labor would be much cheaper in a (assumed to be technologically
stagnant) society where there were no consumer goods besides the one
fiber everyone wears, the one staple food everyone eats, the minimal
cave shelter, et cetera.

One could not build the pyramids with stone tools in a society where
three days's pay at minimum wage can net you enough money to buy a
walkman.

I'm sure I'm reinventing something from basic economics here. What is
it?

