> stagnant) society where there were no consumer goods besides the one
> fiber everyone wears, the one staple food everyone eats, the minimal
> cave shelter, et cetera.

  Another important thing to consider is that the Nile region was fertile for a
very few months a year, and the rest of the year there was no real need for a
massive workforce.  The pyramids and other engineering of Egypt were as much
"public works" programs as anything else.  And of course, a busy population is
usually too busy to cause the rulers any problems.

  Hmn.  I have some good references at home, but I can't recall the titles or
authors of a single one at the moment...  There have been a large number of
books written on Egyptian economics/politics/engineering/etc, however.  They
should be fairly easy to find with a library search.

> One could not build the pyramids with stone tools in a society where
> three days's pay at minimum wage can net you enough money to buy a
> walkman.

  Also note that a single modern worker is far more productive than a single
Egyptian worker was, because we have better tools and techniques (not by a lot,
possibly, compared to the Egyptians at their height...).  The tools cost money,
the training to use those tools costs money, the materials cost money.  The
Pharoah owned the quarries the stone for the pyramid came from - in the modern
world someone else almost always owns the materials you need.

  Sorry I can't help with the economics term, which is what you wanted, but I
know little about formal economics...  I know a bit more about the ancient
world.

