From: EdRegis@aol.com
Date: Sun, 14 Aug 94 12:53:07 EDT
Subject: [#94-8-158] EPIST: certainty again

Eric Watt Forste (Fri, Aug 12, 1994 9:19 AM EST) writes:

>I agree with you that 100% certainty is possible, but only
>when I keep in mind that if you can get only (for example)
>three significant figures, 100% is the same number as 
>(for example) 99.96%.  The same number, when you're talking
>about the real world, sensations, perceptions, measurements,
>in short, the domain of empirical science. I don't think 100.0%
>certainty is possible if one insists on an infinite number of
>significant zeros after that decimal point.

I've come across this argument a few times and I've never bought it, even in
the realm of number theory, where I guess it originated.  My problem with it
is that the argument is reiterable for any number less than 100, so that if
you keep on applying it consistently, pretty soon you find that there's
really no difference betwen 0 [zero] and 100, or, indeed, between any two
numbers at all.  

If, for example, you argued that y is the same number as z because y
approaches z asymptotically, then you could equally well argue the same thing
regarding the next nearest number below y (say x), that as long as x
approaches y asymptotically, then it's really the same number as y.  E.g.: 

z: 100.000...
y:  99.999...
x:  99.998..., and so on, seriatim, back to zero, so that all numbers are
really the same number, without excepton.  

I see this as a mathematical version of a slippery slope argument, one that
minimizes the difference between two things that are actually different
although substantially similar.  Once you get started on that train of
reasoning, there's really no stopping it.     

Attempting to apply that sort of reasoning to everyday life, on the principle
that as far as "the real world" is concerned we just have to tolerate some
inherent sloppiness and ambiguity, well, I don't buy that either.   To me,
it's just a way of trying to have your cake and eat it too.  Analytical
distinctions can and should be maintained wherever they exist.   


