Subject: [#94-8-159] PHIL: Logician, heal thyself

Craig Presson (Fri, Aug 12, 1994 4:36 PM EST) writes: 

>[...] this is the starting point of Greek philosophy, wasn't it? >The most
radical skeptics like Epaminides (who would hardly
>even talk to anyone), insisted that no one could know anything
>because your only inputs are sensory and no one can prove the
>objective world is not an illusion. Elaborate and oddly
>self-consistent as illusions go, but nevertheless ...  

Hell, yes!  All of this was fully discussed and the issues entirely well
understood by the ancient Greeks.  The most consistent and honest of the
radical skeptics was Cratylus, who was the one who never spoke; instead, he
crooked his finger.  He was not entirely consistent here, however, because
(as others have pointed out since) even crooking his finger implied he knew
that this was a symbol that had a meaning, a symbol that others could attach
the same meaning to it, and so on.  

For my money, radical skepticism is a position that can be maintained as a
belief, but *only* as a belief, in the same way that religious beliefs can be
maintained.  You don't need any evidence for a belief, you can believe any
damn thing you want.  

Radical skepticism, however, is not a position that anyone could possibly
give a sound argument for, and here's why: Any such argument would require
one or more premises.  Either those premisses are known to be true or they
are not known to be true.  If they are *not* known to be true, then the
argument doesn't even get started.  If the premises *are* known to be true,
then at least *that* much is known to be true, so that we do have some
genuine knowledge after all.  So you can't really prove skepticism, you can
only believe it, just as if it were the secular religion that it in fact is.
 

>You guys are making my head hurt.
 
I can see it.  Murray Gell-Mann, the physicist, once said in an Omni
interview that he had a doctor's prescription against arguing with
philosophers.  He used to talk with philosphers and they'd tell him how
impossible it was for him, or for scientists, or for anyone, to know anything
at all, about anything whatsoever, and this used to make his blood boil.  

I have to ally myself with Gell-Mann on this issue.  In fact, I find it
rather ironic that just as physicists are closing on what just *might* turn
out to be a complete and final theory of nature, the famous and controversial
TOE, or Theory of Everything, the so-called "philosophers of science" are
still sitting on the sidelines helpfully informing us about how absolutely
impossible it is for anyone to know the least little thing.  And they do this
in the name of enlightened thinking!  

It was partly because of my own contempt for this charming attribute of some
philosophers that I left the discipline in favor of writing about science and
scientists.  (It was the philosophers, don't forget, who were maintaining,
even in the late 1950s, that atoms didn't really exist and could never be
proven to exist.  What a swell bunch!)  

